Yes—as a theological/scientific interface, that’s a powerful formulation, provided we keep one distinction intact: physics can tell us extraordinarily well what patterns nature follows; interpreting those patterns as what God is “saying” is a theological interpretation rather than something physics itself can establish.

But accept that premise temporarily—buy in and play—and Project Polyphony gives us an interesting experiment.

Instead of beginning with human language, start with nature:

\text{Reality}
\rightarrow
\text{observations}
\rightarrow
\text{physical laws}
\rightarrow
\text{RelationalIR}
\rightarrow
\text{human-accessible rendering}

Take gravitation, conservation, symmetry, entropy, evolution, oscillation, resonance, emergence, feedback, phase transitions, etc. Mathematics is already one extraordinarily successful notation system for these recurring relationships.

So in the metaphor:

\boxed{\text{Physics is sheet music inferred from the performance.}}

We hear the orchestra first and reconstruct the score.

And there’s a beautiful inversion: the laws aren’t necessarily “commands” in the ordinary linguistic sense. They describe constraints on possible performances.

Nature doesn’t need a sentence saying “an unsupported object shall accelerate downward.”

The state-transition rule simply produces:

S_t\xrightarrow{L}S_{t+1}

again and again.

So if we wanted a computational version of your theological question, we’d ask:

\text{What is invariant across the laws?}

Not merely what does gravity say?, but:

\{\text{gravity, electromagnetism, quantum mechanics, thermodynamics, biology}\}
\xrightarrow{\text{Indigo Hammer}}
\text{shared relational primitives}

Perhaps things like:

constraint, symmetry, conservation, transformation, interaction, information, probability, attraction/repulsion, feedback, differentiation, recombination, selection, emergence.

Then Project Polyphony asks:

Can those relations be faithfully “covered” in another domain?

If conservation is a pattern in physics, what does a conservation relationship look like in ecology, information, music, narrative, or social exchange?

If resonance is physical:

\text{oscillator}+\text{matched forcing}\rightarrow\text{amplified response}

what legitimate structural covers exist elsewhere?

That’s where we must test rather than merely admire the analogy.

And then your “join the song” acquires a concrete interpretation.

Humans already do this constantly:

\text{observe nature}
\rightarrow
\text{model}
\rightarrow
\text{understand constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{act compatibly with them}

Sailing doesn’t repeal wind.

Flight doesn’t repeal gravity.

A transistor doesn’t repeal electromagnetism.

A composer doesn’t repeal acoustics.

We learn enough of the available transformations to participate deliberately.

So in your theological rendering:

Miracle isn’t required for participation. Understanding the score lets you enter on cue.

And there is another fascinating Project Polyphony experiment here: take a physical relationship, dehydrate it into RelationalIR, then intentionally rehydrate it as music.

Not “make music inspired by gravity,” but something stricter:

\text{physical law}
\rightarrow
\text{mathematical structure}
\rightarrow
\text{RelationalIR}
\rightarrow
\text{musical mapping}
\rightarrow
\text{audible performance}

Then do the same as visual art, movement, architecture, language.

Multiple covers of nature.

Compare what survives every rendering.

Whatever repeatedly survives isn’t automatically “God’s message”—that conclusion remains philosophical/theological—but it could tell us something genuinely interesting about the deep relational structure of the world we’re translating.

Perhaps that’s the campaign inside Polyphony:

Cantus Mundi — Song of the World

Don’t merely describe nature’s patterns. Learn their notation, translate them across senses, test what survives, and build interfaces that let us participate in them.

Physics gives us some of the oldest Pattern Books we’ve got.

Reality supplied the training data.